{
  "id": "naive-bayes-bernoulli",
  "title": "Naive Bayes Log-Likelihood (Bernoulli)",
  "description": "Train a Bernoulli Naive Bayes model on binary features and return the unnormalized log posterior for every test sample and class.\n\n$$\n\\theta_{jc}=\\frac{N_{jc}+1}{N_c+2}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\log \\widetilde{P}(c\\mid\\mathbf{x})=\\log P(c)+\\sum_{j=1}^{D}\\left[x_j\\log\\theta_{jc}+(1-x_j)\\log(1-\\theta_{jc})\\right]\n$$\n\nHere, $N_c$ is the number of training samples in class $c$, $N_{jc}$ counts class-$c$ samples whose feature $j$ is one, and $D$ is the feature count. The added one and two implement Laplace smoothing. Order output columns by ascending class label, round values to four decimals, and return a NumPy array of shape (n_test, n_classes).",
  "seoDescription": "Compute Bernoulli Naive Bayes log-likelihoods from binary features, class priors, and feature probabilities.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: X_train = [[1, 0], [0, 1]], y_train = [1, 0], X_test = [[1, 0]]\n\nOutput: [[-2.8904, -1.5041]]\n\nExplanation: The columns correspond to classes 0 and 1, and the observed feature pattern is more likely under class 1.",
    "Input: X_train = [[1, 0], [1, 1], [0, 0], [0, 1]], y_train = [0, 0, 1, 1], X_test = [[1, 0], [0, 1]]\n\nOutput: [[-1.674, -2.7726], [-2.7726, -1.674]]"
  ],
  "requirements": "* Estimate class priors from training frequencies\n* Apply Laplace smoothing to every Bernoulli feature probability\n* Include contributions from both present and absent features in log space\n* Return a NumPy array rounded to four decimals",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: Naive Bayes Log-Likelihood (Bernoulli)\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nReal-world data is inherently stochastic and incomplete. Probability theory and statistics provide principled methods to quantify uncertainty, model data-generating distributions, and evaluate confidence in empirical predictions. **Naive Bayes Log-Likelihood (Bernoulli)** computes statistical moments, likelihoods, or uncertainty metrics across observation samples.\n\nIn this problem, the objective is to implement **Naive Bayes Log-Likelihood (Bernoulli)** efficiently and accurately adhering to standard mathematical conventions.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe operation is governed by the following mathematical formulation:\n\n$$\n\\theta_{jc}=\\frac{N_{jc}+1}{N_c+2}\n$$\n\nEvery variable and index in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1: Input Validation & Pre-processing**: Validate tensor dimensions, convert inputs into contiguous array buffers, and extract structural shape parameters.\n2. **Phase 2: Core Transformation**: Apply vectorized operations directly across array dimensions without explicit Python loops.\n3. **Phase 3: Aggregation & Normalization**: Compute required summations, projections, or element-wise bounds while safeguarding against numerical instability.\n4. **Phase 4: Output Formatting**: Ensure the resulting array or dictionary matches expected output data types and precision tolerances.\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Log-Space Calculations**: Probabilities can be extremely small; computing log-likelihoods prevents floating-point underflow when multiplying many probability densities.\n* **Maximum Likelihood Estimation**: Many machine learning loss functions (like Cross-Entropy and Mean Squared Error) directly derive from minimizing negative log-likelihood under specific distributional assumptions.\n* **Outlier Sensitivity**: High-order moments (skewness and kurtosis) reveal distribution heavy-tails, signaling when robust statistics or clipping are necessary.",
