  "solution": "### 💡 Intuition & Approach\nVectorized, numerically stable reference implementation following exact specifications.\n\n### 💻 Reference Implementation\n```python\nimport math\nimport numpy as np\n\ndef naive_bayes_bernoulli(X_train: list, y_train: list, X_test: list) -> np.ndarray:\n    Xt = np.array(X_train, dtype=int)\n    yt = np.array(y_train, dtype=int)\n    Xte = np.array(X_test, dtype=int)\n    classes = np.unique(yt)\n    classes.sort()\n    stats = {}\n    N = len(Xt)\n    for c in classes:\n        Xc = Xt[yt == c]\n        prior = len(Xc) / N\n        p_feat = (np.sum(Xc, axis=0) + 1.0) / (len(Xc) + 2.0)\n        stats[c] = (prior, p_feat)\n    all_logps = []\n    for x in Xte:\n        logps = []\n        for c in classes:\n            prior, p_feat = stats[c]\n            logp = math.log(prior) + np.sum(x * np.log(p_feat) + (1 - x) * np.log(1.0 - p_feat))\n            logps.append(round(float(logp), 4))\n        all_logps.append(logps)\n    return np.array(all_logps)\n```\n\n### ⏱️ Complexity Analysis\n- **Time Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ where $N$ represents the input dimensions.\n- **Space Complexity**: $\\mathcal{O}(N)$ for memory buffers.\n",
